WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.370
BUKOLA AYODELE: Many
cloud-native development teams

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:06.210
work with a mix of configuration
systems, APIs, and tools

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.400
to manage their infrastructure.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.860
This mix is often
difficult to understand,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.700
leading to reduced velocity
and expensive mistakes.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.820
Let's look at how Alice
uses Config Connector

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.220
for consistent GCP
resource management.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.330
Alice is a new engineer
preparing for her first

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.560
on call.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.890
She is also worried about
having to learn new commands

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.110
to make configuration changes.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.530
Using specialized tools to
solve a plethora of problems

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.530
is one of the benefits of
the Linux and microservices

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.700
approaches, but it also
comes with added complexity

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.780
of having to learn many tools
to do, often, similar tasks.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.900
Many times, it turns out that
these separate components

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.140
would have been served if they
allowed you to express what you

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.620
want to do without having
to focus on the specific API

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.460
to make it happen.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.640
Luckily, Alice has a lot of
experience with K8 toolsheets.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.430
She discovers that
using Config Connecter,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.710
cloud resources are
managed consistently

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:12.840
with K8's native resources,
like pods and services.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.850
Alice realizes
that she can fully

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.540
operate the cloud using
her existing K8 expertise

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.850
and getops.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.040
And that's the benefit
of Config Connecter.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.590
Config Connector is
a Kubernetes add-on

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.470
that allows you to manage
Google Cloud resources

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.660
through Kubernetes' manifests.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.400
Because you can manage your
Google Cloud infrastructure

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.210
the same way you manage
your Kubernetes application,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.390
complexity and cognitive load
is reduced for developers.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.140
This means more focus
where it matters--

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.150
on the infrastructure.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.370
Config Connectors
provide a collection

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.930
of Kubernetes' custom
resource definitions,

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.590
CRDs, and controllers.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.790
The Config Connector CRDs allow
Kubernetes to create and manage

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.600
Google Cloud resources,
like a Cloud SQL database,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.490
the same way Kubernetes
developers are

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.180
used to applying Kubernetes'
objects to their clusters.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.080
You configure and apply
objects to your clusters.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.110
Since Alice is already familiar
with Kubernetes' tooling

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.500
and declarative workflow,
she is able to use

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.740
her existing expertise to
quickly ramp up and manage

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.690
the customer's workload.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.470
Let's see a quick example
of this in action.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.930
Alice wants to create a
storage bucket to store images.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.130
The first step is to create
a resource definition, which

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.710
uses YAML to specify
the storage bucket's

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.960
information, like
its name and what

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.360
project it's associated with.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.750
This resource can be updated
through the Kubernetes API

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.780
by updating the storage
buckets YAML file.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.850
In this case,
Alice added a label

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.360
to the bucket, an annotation
to specify other behaviors

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.830
on her storage bucket.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.920
Running the familiar
kubectl commands we already

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.680
know and love, like
kubectl apply in this case,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.950
we can create the
storage bucket.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.800
Now, instead of having
to learn another tool,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.940
like the gsutil tool,
Alice was able to use

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.180
the knowledge she already had
to create her storage bucket.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.190
All these changes were done
through the Kubernetes API,

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.180
which means Alice could apply
the expertise she's already

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.190
built up over time
without having

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.250
to learn new tools to
manage infrastructure.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.280
That's one of the benefits
of managing GCP resources

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.680
with Config Connecter.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.410
In combination with
Config Sync, you also

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.770
ensure the change process is
consistent through GitOps,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.800
removing the risk
of config drift.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.720
So that's it for
Config Connecter.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.820
Make sure to check out
the description for links

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.610
to more resources on it,
and like and subscribe

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.790
to the channel for more
videos about Google Cloud.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:48.810
Thanks for watching.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:52.160
